        items: [
          {title:'Philadelphia composting drop-off sites \u2014 where and what\u2019s accepted', sub:'The Philly Compost Initiative operates drop-off sites at several farmers markets and community gardens. Accepts fruit and vegetable scraps, coffee grounds, and eggshells. Meat and dairy not accepted. Find sites at phila.gov/compost.', tag:'Resource'},
          {title:'How to start a block-level composting program in your neighborhood', sub:'The Sanitation Convenience Centers accept green waste year-round. Block associations can apply to the Streets Department for a communal composting bin through the Community Composting Program. Minimal paperwork required.', tag:'How-to'},
          {title:'Philadelphia food waste by the numbers \u2014 what your neighbors throw away', sub:'The City estimates Philadelphia residents send over 250,000 tons of food waste to landfill annually. Reducing food waste is the single highest-impact climate action available to most households, per EPA estimates.', tag:'Guide'}
